Commentary of Ibn Sa'di (Taysir al-Karim al-Rahman) on surah 29, verses 28–35:

{28–29} Then Allah sent Lut (Lot) to his people. Along with their polytheism, they had combined the commission of the foul deed among males, the cutting off of the road (highway robbery), and the spread of evil practices in their gatherings. Lut advised them concerning these matters and made clear to them the ugliness of these things in themselves and the severe punishment to which they lead, but they did not turn back and did not take heed. {So the answer of his people was only that they said: "Bring us the punishment of Allah, if you are of the truthful."}

{30–35} Their prophet despaired of them, knew that they deserved the punishment, and was distressed by the severity of their denial of him, so he invoked against them, and {said: "My Lord, help me against the corrupt people."} Allah answered his supplication and sent the angels to destroy them. They passed by Ibrahim (Abraham) before that, and gave him the good news of Ishaq (Isaac), and after Ishaq, Ya'qub (Jacob). Then Ibrahim asked them: where are they heading? They informed him that they were going to destroy the people of Lut, so he began to argue with them and say: {"Indeed, in it is Lut."} They said to him: {"We will surely save him and his family, except his wife; she was among those who remain behind."} Then they went on until they came to Lut, and his coming distressed him and he was overcome with anxiety on their account, to the extent that he did not recognize them, and thought they were among the travelers, the guests, so he feared for them from his people. They said to him: {"Do not fear and do not grieve."} and informed him that they were the messengers of Allah. {"Indeed, we will save you and your family, except your wife; she was among those who remain behind. Indeed, we will send down upon the people of this town a scourge"}, i.e., a punishment {"from the sky, because they used to defy (Allah)."} They commanded him to travel with his family by night. When morning came, Allah overturned their dwellings, making its upper part its lower part, and rained upon them stones of baked clay (sijjil), in succession, until He annihilated and destroyed them, and they became tales told and lessons learned. {And We have certainly left of it a clear sign for a people who reason}, i.e., We left from the dwellings of the people of Lut clear traces for people who understand the lessons with their hearts and so benefit from them, as Allah the Exalted said: {"And indeed, you pass by them in the morning, and at night. Will you not then reason?"}

